WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.710
- Hello there, my name's Gavin,

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:03.430
and welcome to this
episode of The Slow Mo Guys

00:00:03.430 --> 00:00:06.000
very oddly presented from my living room.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.787
A while ago, I made a video called

00:00:07.787 --> 00:00:09.450
"How a Camera Works In Slow Mo",

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.040
and the response was great.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.860
So I thought a good natural
progression to that video

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:16.050
would be how a TV works in slow mo.

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:18.920
This is an 85-inch LCD TV,

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:20.600
and whenever I'm playing something on it

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:22.000
or watching something on it,

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.350
my eyes and brain are
being misled and tricked,

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:28.110
giving me the illusion of
watching a moving object

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:31.360
when in fact I'm just
watching several still images

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:33.430
just shown to me very, very fast.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:34.510
If I'm watching a film,

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:38.610
I'm being shown 24 images
every second, and to my eyes,

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:40.270
that looks like I'm
looking at a moving object

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:43.700
when in fact I'm looking
at 24 individual pictures.

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.270
If I'm playing a game, it's the same,

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:48.610
except maybe 30 to 60 frames a second,

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:50.456
and if I'm on a PC, it could be 100s,

00:00:50.456 --> 00:00:51.430
PC master race.

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:53.980
But a TV like this is actually incapable

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:57.810
of showing you one image and
then 1/24 of a second later

00:00:57.810 --> 00:00:59.810
just switching all at
once to the next image,

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:01.640
and to illustrate this next point,

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:06.640
I'm gonna use a very old
and very crap CRT TV.

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:08.560
That stands for cathode ray tube.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:10.570
If you've ever seen one of these filmed,

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:13.480
you may notice that it looks
slightly different on camera

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:14.913
than it does to your eye.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:17.400
Look at that.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:18.770
The reason it looks like this

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:22.960
is because the shutter speed
of this camera is out of sync

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:25.340
with the refresh rate of this screen.

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:28.860
The frame is constructed
from the top to the bottom

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.160
multiple times per second;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.200
that's 60 times in the US, 60 Hertz.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.110
I've prepared for you
some high speed footage

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:36.630
that I shot a long time ago on the v2511

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:40.220
of this screen and this
screen and some others.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.370
A lot of it is Dan paying "Super Mario"

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:44.310
on the NES extremely badly.

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:46.360
Here's the TV and the
cat played back at 25;

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.910
this is how it would be
perceived in real time.

00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:50.580
And now at 1600 frames a second,

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:52.500
you can actually see the scan line moving

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:53.710
from the top to the bottom,

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:56.010
and you'll notice that on a CRT screen,

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:59.020
it's only the active line
of pixels that's bright,

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:00.440
and your persistence of vision

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:03.610
will actually build that
into a complete image.

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:05.199
It's messing with my eyes, this.

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:08.623
- It's like a dance floor.

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:17.140
Oh, I didn't even make
it past the first guy.

00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:22.760
- Slowed all the way down
to 2500 frames a second,

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:25.910
you can now differentiate
each individual frame

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:27.563
being built from top to bottom.

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:35.100
It takes an extremely fast camera

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.530
to see that each frame
is built line by line

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:41.510
from top to bottom, but it
takes an even faster camera

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:45.320
to see that each line is
drawn from left to right.

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:47.810
Slowed down to 28,500 frames a second,

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:49.260
we're now seeing glimpses of that,

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:51.293
but we do need to go even slower.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:55.730
This is now 118,000 frames a second,

00:02:55.730 --> 00:02:57.480
and I'm gonna put the
stats up for you here

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.960
so you can see the actual
amount of time that it took,

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:02.290
and you can see now that
the line is being drawn

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:04.420
from left to right on the screen.

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:07.490
Now at 146,000 frames a second,

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:10.550
to gain perspective on just
how slow this actually is,

00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:13.030
you can see the exact time I shot this.

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:15.210
So this is hours, it
was just past midnight,

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:19.490
23 minutes, 41 seconds,
this is 1/10 of a second,

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:23.910
1/100 of a second, 1/1000 of
a second or a millisecond,

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:26.718
and then over here you've
got 1/10,000 of a second,

00:03:26.718 --> 00:03:29.440
1/100,000 of a second, and this unit here

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:33.070
is the millionth of a
second, or a microsecond.

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:36.760
We are now at 380,000 frames a second

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:38.040
as our recording frame rate.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:40.400
That is the highest frame
rate we've ever shot

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:42.810
so far on this channel,
and using this information,

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:44.600
here's a little bonus fact:

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:48.430
a CRT screen can draw Mario's mustache

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:53.330
in less than 1/380,000 of a second.

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:56.070
That is some seriously fast facial hair.

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:57.430
And if you're wondering why this footage

00:03:57.430 --> 00:03:59.760
looks extremely mucky and blurry,

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:03.410
it's because the resolution
is only 256 by 128,

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:06.563
which, plonked into a
4K frame, is this big.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:10.610
That CRT screen is standard def;

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:15.610
this is a 4K screen, which
means it's 3840 by 2160 pixels.

00:04:17.710 --> 00:04:19.390
That's over eight million pixels.

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:23.420
So think of the processing
power that this TV has to have

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:27.290
to update an image that big
that many times every second.

00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:29.500
The first thing you'll notice
about a modern LCD screen

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:31.830
is that it's not only
the active line of pixels

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:34.430
that retains brightness;
it's the entire image.

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:36.350
So you can actually see the full image

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:38.320
as each scan-line passes down the screen.

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:42.427
This is every frame of the
start-up sequence on an Xbox One.

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:53.240
I also recorded myself
playing a game of "Halo".

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:55.410
Nothing will make you feel
worse about your performance

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:58.760
than watching your lousy aim
in slow mo, look at that.

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:00.490
It's crazy to think that
when you're playing "Halo",

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:03.060
this is actually what
is happening on your TV.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:04.440
If only you could see at this speed,

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:06.698
your aim would be incredible.

00:05:06.698 --> 00:05:08.120
It honestly makes me feel bad

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:10.040
when I fall asleep watching TV,

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:13.000
knowing that this TV is
doing all this intensive work

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:17.600
changing literally 10s of
millions of pixels every second,

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:19.020
and there's no one there watching it.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:21.810
Here's a fun fact: the
same applies to an iPhone,

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:24.350
except it's in portrait mode.

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:26.020
So if you're watching a video

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:28.260
in landscape mode on your iPhone,

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:30.120
you're actually getting
updates from left to right,

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:33.010
or right to left, depending on
which way you've flipped it,

00:05:33.010 --> 00:05:33.980
and that just proved to me

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:35.690
that you can't see the refresh direction

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:37.520
with your naked eye,
because I had no idea,

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:39.910
whenever I was watching a
YouTube video on my iPhone,

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:40.920
that the screen was updating

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:42.530
in a completely different direction.

00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:44.600
I'm not sure if this is the
case for all smartphones,

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:46.830
but it's certainly the
case on an iPhone 7 Plus,

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:48.100
which is what I filmed this on.

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:49.980
So we've talked about one illusion of TVs,

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:51.360
the illusion of movement.

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:53.240
The second illusion I wanna talk about

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:54.540
is the illusion of color.

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:57.250
For this next part, I'm
gonna need a second camera.

00:05:57.250 --> 00:06:00.050
Here's one, that's you, hello, you.

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:03.210
So in order to film this
screen extremely close,

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:04.710
I'm gonna have to set my focus

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:06.260
to the minimum possible distance,

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:08.180
so it's sort of like right here now.

00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:10.850
Set to my minimum focus, as I slowly move

00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:12.760
towards the screen, it becomes sharper,

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:14.220
and you will then, at the last minute,

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:16.653
see a very odd looking pattern.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:20.390
And what you're seeing there is an effect

00:06:20.390 --> 00:06:22.820
caused by the camera, this camera,

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:25.340
trying to resolve individual pixels

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:27.990
on the surface of this screen.

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:31.380
As I push further forward,
the effect disappears,

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:32.890
and everything goes out of focus.

00:06:32.890 --> 00:06:36.150
That's because I'm now beyond
the minimum focal distance

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:38.993
of this lens, which is about,
well, that's about there.

00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:41.500
Not close enough.

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:44.223
In order to get closer, I'm
gonna need a macro lens.

00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:47.840
Here's one.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:49.880
As I approach the white,

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:52.053
and everything starts to become in focus,

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:56.960
you can see that white
isn't so white anymore.

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:00.080
It looks like I have to
go closer even than that.

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:02.810
Thankfully, I can go all the way

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:05.270
to five times magnification.

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:06.380
Now, one thing from this point,

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:08.100
I am definitely gonna need a tripod,

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:10.240
because there's no way my arms

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:11.850
are sturdy enough to hold this in place,

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:14.250
but I'll just ease it in just to show you

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:16.783
the level of magnification
we're talking about now.

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:24.700
Well, this close, it's a
different story altogether.

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:25.820
I'm gonna get a tripod.

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:26.653
Here's one.

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:29.880
What a mission this is, all right,

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:31.250
let's see what we can do here.

00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:33.200
We're so close up right now

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:35.070
that I can actually disturb this image

00:07:35.070 --> 00:07:36.623
by blowing on the lens.

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:41.630
We're now looking at the sub-pixel level.

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:44.630
A pixel is made up of three sub-pixels,

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:48.010
red, green, and blue, RGB, you
may have heard that before,

00:07:48.010 --> 00:07:51.540
and this creates the
illusion of different colors.

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:54.520
By dimming and brightening
different sub-pixels

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:57.300
to different intensities,
this screen can create

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:01.250
the illusion of literally
millions of different colors.

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:04.660
When all three are lit to full
brightness, you get white.

00:08:04.660 --> 00:08:06.490
When all three dim, you go through gray

00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:07.630
all the way down to black.

00:08:07.630 --> 00:08:10.670
So if green dims away, and
red and blue are still lit,

00:08:10.670 --> 00:08:13.080
then you go into magenta,
purple, that sort of area,

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:14.460
and that's how the colors are made.

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:18.970
So every time on your TV you're
looking at a white image,

00:08:18.970 --> 00:08:23.920
you're looking at tons of
blue, green, and red lights.

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:27.940
They're just so small they
look like white to your eye.

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:30.480
Before you get white;
red, green, and blue blurs

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:32.770
into yellow, cyan, and magenta,

00:08:32.770 --> 00:08:33.610
and that's what happens here

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:35.860
when I move the screen
slightly out of focus.

00:08:38.270 --> 00:08:40.793
This is too close to watch
a Slow Mo Guys video.

00:08:41.810 --> 00:08:42.693
I might vomit.

00:08:44.020 --> 00:08:45.593
It's the same situation,

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:49.283
just entire blocks and they're bigger.

00:08:50.590 --> 00:08:53.530
As I mentioned before,
this is a 4K LCD screen,

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:55.810
which, a while back, were typically lit

00:08:55.810 --> 00:09:00.680
by CCFLs, or a cold
cathode fluorescent lamp,

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:03.330
which means the entire panel is backlit

00:09:03.330 --> 00:09:04.990
by fluorescent tubes.

00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:08.760
Nowadays, they are backlit by LEDs.

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:12.550
This is why this would actually
be marketed as an LED TV.

00:09:12.550 --> 00:09:15.560
The benefit of LED
screens over CCFL screens

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:17.250
is that they're a lot thinner.

00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:20.040
Now I'm gonna point it at
where some black text is.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:23.640
Now, interestingly, even
though this area is black

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:26.160
because all the sub-pixels have dimmed,

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:28.180
they are still in fact backlit.

00:09:28.180 --> 00:09:30.395
Let me show you that right now.

00:09:30.395 --> 00:09:31.720
Now you can see, as we push in here,

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:32.830
you have to pardon the noise,

00:09:32.830 --> 00:09:34.130
we're at an extremely high ISO

00:09:34.130 --> 00:09:36.320
to get this shot through a macro lens,

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:38.130
but you can see even the dimmed pixels,

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:39.750
part of the liquid crystal display

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:41.570
as it's now trying to block all light

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:44.820
from penetrating through, but
it's still a backlit pixel,

00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:46.830
and that's one of the
fundamental limitations

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:48.230
of an LCD screen.

00:09:48.230 --> 00:09:50.480
You can see this effect on an LCD screen

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:51.600
in the credits of a film,

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:53.290
'cause you've got an almost
completely black image,

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:54.770
but because there's white text,

00:09:54.770 --> 00:09:57.760
the entire backlight has to
be on to display the white,

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:00.620
which means light will leak
out from the black pixels,

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:02.800
which means it's not true black.

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:03.750
There is another technology

00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:05.510
that's becoming much
more common these days,

00:10:05.510 --> 00:10:09.240
and that is an OLED screen,
organic light-emitting diode.

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:10.620
I did wanna include a comparison

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:13.020
between an LCD panel and an OLED panel,

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:15.410
but I didn't actually have an OLED TV,

00:10:15.410 --> 00:10:17.530
and by sheer luck, right in the middle

00:10:17.530 --> 00:10:19.130
of me shooting all this footage,

00:10:19.130 --> 00:10:22.890
LG got in touch and offered
to supply me with an OLED TV

00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:24.140
for the purpose of making this video,

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:25.610
which I really appreciate.

00:10:25.610 --> 00:10:26.570
Thanks, LG.

00:10:26.570 --> 00:10:28.150
Why don't we go and take a look at it.

00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:31.800
This is a 77-inch LG OLED TV.

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:33.540
The way OLED technology works is

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:36.110
that each pixel is self-illuminating,

00:10:36.110 --> 00:10:38.300
depending on how much voltage
is passing through it,

00:10:38.300 --> 00:10:41.540
which means there's no
global backlight on the TV.

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:45.600
Each pixel is individually in
control of how bright it is,

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:47.260
and it's not being lit from behind.

00:10:47.260 --> 00:10:50.830
And that means, when we go
to our high ISO experiment,

00:10:50.830 --> 00:10:52.200
just like we did on the LCD,

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:55.100
that when there's an area
of black on the screen,

00:10:55.100 --> 00:10:57.220
all of those pixels are off,

00:10:57.220 --> 00:10:59.930
and you can see here where
I'm putting my cursor

00:10:59.930 --> 00:11:00.790
in front of the lens,

00:11:00.790 --> 00:11:02.850
you can see each sub-pixel lighting up

00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:05.200
and then completely turning
off when it goes away.

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:08.150
This technology means much deeper blacks,

00:11:08.150 --> 00:11:11.490
and the possibility of a very thin screen.

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:13.810
And there you have it: a brief explanation

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:15.800
of how a TV works in slow mo.

00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:17.460
If you found that video interesting,

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:20.090
chances are you might find
some other videos interesting

00:11:20.090 --> 00:11:22.930
on this channel, so make sure you boop,

00:11:22.930 --> 00:11:26.054
and once you've booped,
feel free to check out our

00:11:26.054 --> 00:11:28.700
it's just there.

00:11:28.700 --> 00:11:30.373
Thank you very much for watching.

00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:33.812
That was good timing, weren't it?

00:11:33.812 --> 00:11:37.397
TV timed out, and now there's fireworks.

